{% set local_problem %}
<p>We've watched Hartselle grow over 20+ years. The <a href="/recent-projects/">historic homes downtown</a> — many with original sectional gutters from the 1950s or earlier — need updates that respect their character while providing modern protection. The newer developments spreading across Morgan County need gutters that can handle the area's demanding rainfall.</p>

<p>Hartselle's mix of town lots and more rural properties creates varied drainage patterns. Agricultural land, open fields, and the terrain throughout Morgan County affect how water moves. Gutter systems need to account for that reality, not just follow a standard playbook.</p>

<p>Traditional sectional gutters — the kind with seams every 10 feet — struggle here. Each seam is a potential leak point that weakens over time. During heavy storms (and we get plenty), water finds these weak spots and starts damaging fascia, siding, and foundations.</p>

<p>Seamless gutters eliminate these problems. Custom-fabricated on-site to fit your home exactly, they have connections only at corners and downspout drops — 80% fewer potential leak points than sectional systems.</p>

<p><strong>Our opinion:</strong> If your Hartselle home has sectional gutters over 12-15 years old and you're seeing seam leaks, replace the whole system. We've done the seam-patching game with our neighbors for years. It buys you 2-3 years at best, then you're calling us again. Replacement costs about the same as two or three patch jobs and actually solves the problem.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set service_explanation %}
<p>Seamless gutters are exactly what they sound like: continuous gutters with no seams except at corners and downspout connections. Unlike sectional gutters (pre-cut 10-foot sections joined together), seamless gutters are fabricated on-site from one continuous roll of aluminum coil — cut to your home's exact run lengths.</p>

<p><strong>What we install:</strong> 6-inch K-style gutters in .032 gauge aluminum. Hidden hangers at 24-inch intervals (tighter than the 32-inch code minimum). 3x4 downspouts positioned every 35-40 linear feet. Everything measured and fabricated right in your driveway.</p>

<p><strong>Why those specs:</strong> 6-inch gutters hold about 40% more water than 5-inch. The .032 gauge is about 20% thicker than builder-grade .027 — it doesn't dent when a ladder bumps it, and it holds shape better over decades. And 24-inch hanger spacing prevents the sagging we see on older systems with 32-inch spacing.</p>

<p><strong>The honest bit:</strong> "Seamless" is slightly marketing. You still have seams at corners and downspout drops. What you eliminate is the seam every 10 feet in the middle of runs — and those mid-run seams are what fail first.</p>
{% endset %}

